  {
   "title": "The Wall That Takes the Rain",
   "mood": "curious",
   "paragraphs": [
    {
     "text": "Reason one. The mountains take the rain. Weather in this part of America comes from the Pacific. Wet air rolls in off the ocean and hits a wall: the Sierra Nevada, a range of granite peaks more than fourteen thousand feet high, right along the California border. The air rises, cools, and drops its water on the western slopes. Snow piles up there by the meter. By the time that air comes down the other side, into Nevada, there is very little left.",
