    },
    {
     "text": "And it is not one wall. Nevada is made of them. Drive across the state and the road climbs a mountain range, drops into a flat dry valley, climbs another range, drops again, over and over, dozens of times. Geologists call it the Basin and Range. Each range squeezes a little more water out of the sky for itself, and each valley floor gets less. The towns that do exist sit where a stream comes down off a mountain, and almost nowhere else.",
