    {
     "text": "It was not always like this. At the end of the last ice age, a lake covered more than eight thousand five hundred square miles of northwestern Nevada, nearly the size of Lake Erie and in places some nine hundred feet deep. Geologists call it Lake Lahontan. You can still see its old shorelines, cut like bathtub rings into the mountainsides. Then the climate dried, and the lake broke into pieces that shrank and disappeared. Only two real lakes are left: Pyramid Lake and Walker Lake.",
