    {
     "text": "Without rivers that run all year, there is little to farm. Most of Nevada's land is too dry, too salty or too steep to grow a crop without water brought in from somewhere else. The valleys that are green are green because of a single stream, a spring, or a well. Everywhere else is sagebrush, and cattle need a lot of it.",
