  {
   "title": "The Silver Ran Out",
   "mood": "tension",
   "paragraphs": [
    {
     "text": "Reason three. The rush came, and then it left. In 1859, prospectors found silver and gold near what became Virginia City: the Comstock Lode. Within fifteen years Virginia City had about twenty-five thousand people, more than any city between Denver and San Francisco. Nevada became a state in 1864, in large part because of that silver. And then the ore ran thin. By 1879 the town had fewer than eleven thousand, and it kept falling.",
