     "text": "Reason four. Most of Nevada was never handed out. When the West was settled, the federal government gave away or sold land to homesteaders, railroads and states. In Nevada, few people wanted dry sagebrush, and most of it stayed with Washington. Today, by the count of Congress's own research service, about eighty percent of Nevada is federal land, the highest share of any state. Most of it is run by the Bureau of Land Management, as open range for grazing, mining and recreation.",
